  "body": "Two small fixes from a manual audit of the previous two releases. **One of them is notable mainly for what it does NOT do.**\n\n## 1. The tag fallback was one-directional\n\nA user who configures `foo` found nothing when the upstream index lists only `foo:free`. The lookup now tries, in order:\n\n```\nexact  ->  tag-stripped  ->  a single tagged variant in the SAME provider bucket\n```\n\nThe third step fires **only when exactly one** allow-listed tagged entry exists. Two or more (`foo:free` *and* `foo:nitro`) return unknown — choosing one would assert a variant the user did not write.\n\n## 2. Client adaptive was recorded as \"on\" — and the obvious fix was wrong\n\nA client asking for Anthropic's adaptive thinking resolved to `control=on`, indistinguishable in the log from a client asking for `enabled`.\n\nThe obvious fix — resolve it to the `ReasoningControl.ADAPTIVE` member added in v5.35.0 — was designed, attempted, and **abandoned**, because `ADAPTIVE` has `requests_reasoning=False`. That property is exactly what makes an adaptive **tier** correctly send nothing to providers with no adaptive channel. Applying it to the **client** path stops those providers being asked to think at all:\n\n```\ngroq         {'reasoning_effort': 'medium'}                     ->  {}\nfireworks    {'reasoning_effort': 'high'}                       ->  {}\nzai          {'thinking': {'type':'enabled', ...}}              ->  {}\nfeatherless  {'chat_template_kwargs': {'enable_thinking':True}} ->  {}\n```\n\n**Claude Code sends `thinking: {\"type\":\"adaptive\"}`.** So that would have been a silent capability regression across most providers, shipped as a logging improvement. v5.35.0's committed baseline snapshot catches it, and an explicit \"prove it or stop\" instruction caught it before that.\n\n**So the resolved policy is deliberately left alone.** Control stays `on`, every encoder keeps sending exactly what it sends today, and the client's own wording is recorded beside it as `client=adaptive` — on the *requested* policy only.\n\nThe test asserts the zero wire delta **directly** rather than by argument: it emits bodies for a client-adaptive and a client-enabled request across five encoder families and requires them to be equal.\n\n## Verification\n\n```\nreverse lookup   single variant found · two variants stay unknown · exact still wins\n                 never crosses buckets · non-allow-listed tags excluded\nmutation: relax the exactly-one rule -> ambiguity test failed by name\nmutation: disable the annotation     -> wire-stability test failed by name\n\nfull suite (clean shell)  4777 passed, 6 pre-existing PowerShell failures\nruff format / ruff check / ty check   clean repo-wide\n```\n\n## Residual risks\n\n**`client=adaptive` is a string convention, not a column.** It rides inside the existing `requested_reasoning` text, so a query for it is a `LIKE`, not an indexed lookup. Deliberate — a column for one provider family's flag would be heavier than the distinction warrants.\n\n**The reverse lookup adds up to six dict probes per candidate.** Bounded and inside an already-memoised per-provider bucket, but it is more work per request than the previous two steps.\n\n## Tracked separately\n\nThe audit also found that **cost / context-length / vision** metadata is matched *across* all providers with collisions resolved arbitrarily by JSON order — 984 of 3873 keys conflict, 240 of them on the vision flag, and a wrong-`False` there makes MCC skip a route that can handle images. Not touched here; it needs its own measurement and release.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_01Y6SPhyApg7trDx44SJqNti\n",
