    "message": "fix(models,analytics): reverse tag lookup, and record client adaptive\n\nTwo small fixes from an audit of the previous two releases.\n\n1. The model-id tag fallback was one-directional. A user who configures\n   `foo` found nothing when the upstream index lists only `foo:free`.\n   The lookup now tries, in order: exact, tag-stripped, then a single\n   tagged variant within the SAME provider bucket. It uses that third\n   step ONLY when exactly one allow-listed tagged entry exists; two or\n   more (`foo:free` and `foo:nitro`) return unknown, because choosing one\n   would assert a variant the user did not write.\n\n2. A client asking for Anthropic's adaptive thinking was recorded as\n   `control=on`, indistinguishable in the request log from a client that\n   asked for `enabled`.\n\nThe obvious fix for (2) -- resolving a client-sent adaptive to the\nReasoningControl.ADAPTIVE member added in v5.35.0 -- was designed,\nattempted, and ABANDONED because it is wrong. ADAPTIVE has\nrequests_reasoning=False, which is exactly what makes an adaptive TIER\ncorrectly send nothing to providers with no adaptive channel. Applying it\nto the client path stops those providers being asked to think at all.\nMeasured on eight encoders:\n\n    groq         {'reasoning_effort': 'medium'}          -> {}\n    fireworks    {'reasoning_effort': 'high'}            -> {}\n    zai          {'thinking': {'type': 'enabled', ...}}  -> {}\n    featherless  {'chat_template_kwargs': {...True}}     -> {}\n\nClaude Code sends thinking {\"type\":\"adaptive\"}, so that would have been a\nsilent capability regression on most providers, dressed as a logging\nimprovement. The committed baseline snapshot from v5.35.0 catches it.\n\nSo the resolved policy is deliberately left alone -- control stays `on`,\nevery encoder keeps sending what it sends today -- and the client's own\nwording is recorded beside it as `client=adaptive` on the requested\npolicy only. The distinction lands in the log with a provably zero wire\ndelta, which the test asserts directly by emitting bodies for a\nclient-adaptive and a client-enabled request across five encoder families\nand requiring them to be equal.\n\nVerification:\n- Reverse lookup: single variant found, two variants stay unknown, exact\n  still wins, never crosses buckets, non-allow-listed tags excluded.\n  Mutation: relaxing the exactly-one rule failed the ambiguity test by\n  name.\n- Recording: mutation of the annotation failed the wire-stability test by\n  name.\n- Full suite on a clean shell: 4777 passed, 6 pre-existing PowerShell\n  installer failures.\n- ruff format, ruff check, ty check clean repo-wide.\n\nNot done: nothing about the resolved control or any provider encoder. The\ncross-provider matching of cost/context/vision metadata, found during the\nsame audit, is tracked separately.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Y6SPhyApg7trDx44SJqNti",
